<Project>

  <!--
    Everything that has to READ a property the project itself sets lives here rather than in
    Directory.Build.props. The SDK imports the .props file at the TOP of a project, before the
    project's own PropertyGroups have been evaluated, so a condition on $(ReleaseTrain) there
    would always see an empty value. This file is imported at the BOTTOM, after the project body,
    which is the only place the declaration below can be observed.
  -->

  <!--
    <ReleaseTrain> is how a project joins a release train, and it is the ONLY place that membership
    is recorded: tools/trains.sh discovers the projects of a train by reading this property out of the
    project files, and tools/packaging/pack.sh packs exactly what it finds. See CONTRIBUTING.md,
    "Releasing", and ADR-0002 for why the trains are partitioned at all.

    Declaring a train is therefore a statement that the project SHIPS, which is what makes it packable.
    Stating it here rather than in each project keeps the two facts from disagreeing — a project
    declaring a train but left unpackable would be silently absent from its own release.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''">
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Microsoft.Sbom.Targets is what makes -p:GenerateSBOM=true embed an SPDX inventory
    (_manifest/spdx_2.2/manifest.spdx.json) in the produced package; pack.sh asserts the manifest is
    present in every .nupkg it builds, so a project that shipped without this reference would fail the
    pack rather than publish an inventory-less package.

    Attached to the train declaration rather than repeated in every publishable project: the two always
    go together, and a new catalog project would otherwise ship without an SBOM until somebody noticed.
    Build-time only (PrivateAssets=all): it never reaches a consumer's dependency graph. The version is
    pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.props.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Sbom.Targets" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The package icon, attached to the train declaration for the same reason the SBOM is: every
    package this repository publishes wants one, and a catalog project joining its train later would
    otherwise ship with the blank placeholder nuget.org shows until somebody noticed.

    The path is anchored on $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory) — this file's own directory, the repository
    root — rather than written relative to the project. A relative "../icon.png" resolves against the
    PROJECT directory, so it would silently mean src/icon.png here and break the day a project moved
    to another depth. NU5019 is the failure, and it fails the pack rather than producing an iconless
    package, but only for whoever runs pack.

    PackagePath="/" places it at the package root, which is where <PackageIcon> resolves it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''">
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <!--
      A project may carry its own icon.png beside its .csproj, and the catalogues do: each shows the
      family mark with the prefix of the rules it mirrors, so a consumer scanning nuget.org can tell
      them apart at the 128px the listing renders.

      The prefixes are deliberately NOT listed here. An enumeration in a comment is a fact that
      ages: this one read "S, CA, SA" when a fourth catalogue arrived, and a reader copying an icon
      from a sibling had nothing to contradict them — the package shipped with another vendor's
      badge and twelve CI checks passed over it. PackageIconTests now holds the rule instead, by
      requiring every catalogue's icon to differ from every other's and from the fallback below.

      Falling back to the repository's own icon rather than requiring one keeps the property this
      file exists to protect: a catalogue project joining its train later ships with the family mark
      rather than with nuget.org's blank placeholder, and gains its own the day somebody draws it.
    -->
    <PackageIconSource Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/icon.png')">$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/icon.png</PackageIconSource>
    <PackageIconSource Condition="'$(PackageIconSource)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)icon.png</PackageIconSource>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''">
    <!-- PackagePath="/" puts it at the package root as icon.png whichever file was chosen, which is
         where <PackageIcon> above resolves it. -->
    <None Include="$(PackageIconSource)" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The catalogue half of ADR-0038's gate. A catalogue ships build/<its own id>.props, NuGet imports
    it for a DIRECT reference and for nothing further out, and the foundation's
    buildTransitive/DiagnosticCatalog.targets reads the property it sets. That asymmetry is what
    bounds the analyzers to the project that asked for a catalogue, instead of every project
    downstream of one.

    Derived rather than declared, for the reason ReleaseTrain is: a catalogue is a packable project
    that depends on the foundation, which is a fact about the project file, so the fourteenth
    catalogue carries the opt-in without anybody remembering it. A list would have to be edited, and
    a catalogue missing from it would look exactly like one whose consumers are checked.

    Both reference kinds are matched because the repository uses both: the thirteen catalogues on
    their own trains take a PackageReference (ADR-0007 forbids anything else across trains), while
    DiagnosticCatalog.Self rides the lib train WITH the foundation and takes a ProjectReference.

    The count is stated because it says which shapes had to be matched, and it is the kind of fact
    that ages: it read "twelve" for as long as there were thirteen. Nothing enforces it — the
    condition below reads the project, never a number — so treat it as prose and not as a list.

    The foundation itself matches neither clause and must not: a package carrying buildTransitive/
    has its build/ folder ignored outright, so the file would be packed and never read. The direct
    consumer of the foundation is recognised inside the gate instead.

    Item conditions rather than a property, because MSBuild evaluates every property before any item
    and a property condition may not read @(PackageReference).
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != '' and ('@(PackageReference->WithMetadataValue('Identity','DiagnosticCatalog'))' != '' or '@(ProjectReference->WithMetadataValue('Filename','DiagnosticCatalog'))' != '')">
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build/CatalogueAnalyzerOptIn.props"
          Pack="true" PackagePath="build/$(PackageId).props" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The package version and the assembly version must say the same thing.

    They are two independent properties, and nothing in the SDK ties them: $(Version) names the
    package, $(AssemblyVersion) is stamped into the binary and is what the CLR binds on. A release
    that sets one and not the other produces a package whose number is right and whose assembly
    identity is not — which no amount of building, testing or packing notices, because every one of
    those steps is internally consistent with itself.

    lib-v1.0.0 shipped exactly that: DiagnosticCatalog 1.0.0 carried an assembly stamped 0.0.0.0
    while DiagnosticCatalog.Self 1.0.0, packed in the same run, was stamped 1.0.0.0 and recorded a
    reference to DiagnosticCatalog 1.0.0.0. The two cannot bind, so a consumer loading a catalogue
    meets a FileNotFoundException naming a version that was published and does not exist. Nothing in
    the pipeline was looking: not the build, not the suite, not verify-consumption.sh, not the SBOM,
    not the provenance attestation. The version a package DECLARES was checked everywhere; the
    version it CONTAINS was checked nowhere.

    Be clear about what this does NOT catch, because it is what actually happened. This compares two
    PROPERTIES of one evaluation, and lib-v1.0.0's bytes were stale rather than misdeclared: a pack
    that skipped the build recomputed both properties, found them in agreement, and numbered an
    assembly an earlier compilation had stamped. No property comparison can see that. What closes
    that hole is tools/packaging/pack.sh compiling what it packs; this target is the second line,
    for the day someone sets AssemblyVersion by hand and the two disagree at the source.

    Both entry points are covered so the check holds wherever the stamp is decided: CoreCompile is
    where it is applied, and GenerateNuspec catches a pack invoked on its own.

    Only the three leading components are compared. A four-part $(AssemblyVersion) against a
    three-part $(Version) is the ordinary case, not a mismatch, and a SemVer pre-release label
    (1.0.0-rc.1) is deliberately outside the comparison: the CLR has nowhere to put it, so the
    assembly of a pre-release legitimately reads 1.0.0.

    A version neither side can parse as three numbers leaves this quiet rather than guessing. That
    is a real gap and a small one — release.yml already refuses any tag that is not SemVer, so the
    shape reaching a release is always one this reads.
  -->
  <Target Name="DiagnosticCatalogVerifyAssemblyVersionMatchesPackageVersion"
          Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''"
          BeforeTargets="CoreCompile;GenerateNuspec">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_DcatPackageVersionCore>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match('$(Version)', '^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+').Value)</_DcatPackageVersionCore>
      <_DcatAssemblyVersionCore>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match('$(AssemblyVersion)', '^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+').Value)</_DcatAssemblyVersionCore>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Error Condition="'$(_DcatPackageVersionCore)' != '' and '$(_DcatAssemblyVersionCore)' != '' and '$(_DcatPackageVersionCore)' != '$(_DcatAssemblyVersionCore)'"
           Code="DCATPACK001"
           Text="$(PackageId) would ship version $(Version) with an assembly stamped $(AssemblyVersion). A consumer binds on the assembly version, so the two must agree on their first three components. Set Version (the release passes -p:Version) rather than AssemblyVersion, and do not set AssemblyVersion by hand." />
  </Target>

</Project>
